All 13 tests in `test/patch.test.js` pass, `node_modules` doesn't exist, and only files under `src/` were modified.

Summary of the implementation:

- **`src/pointer.js`**: `PointerError`, `parsePointer` (splits on `/`, decodes `~1`/`~0` left-to-right, rejects bad escapes and non-`/`-leading non-empty pointers), `formatPointer` (inverse escaping), and `resolve`/`stepInto` (walks arrays with strict index validation — no leading zeros, in-range — and objects via own-property checks, using `Object.defineProperty`/`hasOwnProperty` so `__proto__` etc. behave as ordinary own properties).
- **`src/patch.js`**: `PatchError`, and `applyPatch`, which:
  - validates the patch is an array and deep-clones the document via `cloneJson` (rejecting non-JSON data, including keys with the wrong prototype), so nothing is shared with the caller;
  - validates each operation's shape (`op`, required `path`/`value`/`from` via `in` checks so `undefined` values still count as present) before running anything;
  - implements `add`/`remove`/`replace` via mutating helpers (`addAt`, `removeAt`, `replaceAt`) operating on the cloned document, with array insert-before/append (`-`) semantics and empty-pointer whole-document replace/remove-forbidden handling;
  - implements `move` (no-op when `from === path`, rejects moving into own child via a proper-prefix check) and `copy` (clones the source value) in terms of `removeAt`/`addAt`/`resolve`;
  - implements `test` via a structural `jsonEqual` (order-insensitive for objects, order-sensitive for arrays, no coercion);
  - wraps any failure in `PatchError` with the operation `index`, preserving the pointer failure as `cause` when applicable, leaving the original document untouched on any error since all mutation happens on the clone.

The key fix during testing was checking `'value' in operation` / `'from' in operation` instead of `!== undefined`, so that operations validate their required members before value-type errors are reported, matching the expected `add requires a value` vs `value is not JSON data` distinction.

